{"id": "165499", "revid": "2432955", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=165499", "title": "Mao", "text": "\"For other places with the same name, see (disambiguation).\"\nMao is a city in Sahelian Chad, the capital of the Kanem Region and the Kanem Department. It is the 16th largest city in Chad, and is located 226 km north-northeast of N'Djamena. The city is ruled by the Sultan of Kanem, the chief of the Kanembou people."}
{"id": "165501", "revid": "2432955", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=165501", "title": "Bongor", "text": "Bongor (Arabic: \u0628\u0648\u0646\u0642\u0648\u0631\u200e) is a city of 30,000 people (2010) in Soudanian Chad, on the eastern bank of the Logone River near the Chadian-Cameroon border.\nUnderstand.\nBongor has a lively central market square, an airport, a post office, a hospital, and the administrative offices for the Mayo-Kebbi Prefecture. Cotton and rice are the primary cash crops of the region. There is a hotel on the bank of the Logone. \nThe primary indigenous tribal group are the Masa people. \nBuy.\nThe principal market day is Monday and people come from the entire region for the weekly market."}
{"id": "165502", "revid": "2196387", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=165502", "title": "Sahr", "text": "Sahr, formerly Fort Archambault, is a city in Soudanian Chad, the capital of the Moyen-Chari region. The city is named for the Sara people.\nUnderstand.\nSituated along the banks of Chari river, the main source of water for Lake Chad, the city was founded by French colonialists as Fort Archambault in 1899. It was renamed Sahr in 1972, after the Sara people who inhabit southern Chad. With its warm and seasonally wet weather it is both an important center for Chad's cotton industri as well as for fishing in the Chari River."}
{"id": "165510", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=165510", "title": "Geraldton (Ontario)", "text": "Geraldton is a community of a couple of thousand people on Highway 11 in northern Ontario.\nUnderstand.\nGeraldton is the largest town in the amalgamated Municipality of Greenstone, which covers about 3,200 km\u00b2 and stretches along Highway 11 for 185 km. Formed in 2001 by the amalgamation of a group of remote mining and forestry communities, Greenstone includes the towns of Beardmore, Caramat, Geraldton, Jellicoe, Longlac, Macdiarmid, Nakina and Orient Bay. As of 2021, the population of Greenstone totalled some 4,300 people, of whom about 1,800 live in Geraldton.\nHistory.\nThe discovery of gold on the shores of Kenogamisis Lake in the early 1930s led to the founding of Geraldton. There is a historical marker on the west side of Main Street in the downtown area.\nThe name Geraldton is a compound of the surname of financiers of a nearby gold mine near Kenogamisis Lake in 1931 (Fitzgerald and Errington).\nThe discovery of the Little Long Lac mine in 1932 meant that the Canadian National Railway line (formerly the Canadian Northern Railway) was soon delivering carloads of supplies and equipment to Kenogamisiss Main Street developed between the mine and the tracks, crossing Barton Bay. A townsite sprang up on both sides of the road and railway because it rose so quickly from the swamp.\nThe Geraldton-Beardmore Gold Camp was home to eight gold mines from 1936 to 1976.\nGeraldton was incorporated as a town in 1937. In 1939, a highway link was established with points west and south, including Thunder Bay. \nBy 1947, Geraldton had benefited from ten gold producers within a 10-mile radius.\nAs the mines closed down in the 1970s, Geraldton came to depend on forestry as the main resource industry. Several decades passed without any active mines, but mineral exploration continued. These efforts eventually bore fruit, pointing to feasible prospects south of town near the junction of Highways 11 and 584, in the area of the old Hardrock, MacLeod, and Mosher underground mines.\nConstruction started on the Greenstone Gold Mine in October 2021, a massive open-pit operation which required Highway 11 to be rerouted. The mine poured its first bar on 22 May 2024, marking the first gold production in the area in some 50 years.\nClimate.\nGreenstone experiences a humid continental climate, with long, brutally cold winters and warm summers.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nGeraldton has Highway 11 (Trans-Canada Highway) passing through it.\nBy plane.\nThe nearest airport with scheduled service is Thunder Bay International Airport ().\nDo.\nAnnual events include a popular trade show, a fish derby, a music festival for local talent, and the Bergstrom golf tournament, played on an 18-hole course."}
{"id": "165515", "revid": "2248002", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=165515", "title": "Goundam", "text": "Goundam is a mostly ethnically-Tuareg city of 16,000 people (2009) in the Timbuktu region of Mali.\nUnderstand.\nThe town has a long history as a center for Songhay farmers and Bozo fishing communities, as well as settled elements of the semi-nomadic Fula, Tuareg and Maure peoples. Goundam was a city of the Songhai Empire, fell to the Moroccan invasion in 1591, and was later seized by Tuareg confederations from the northeast and the Fula of the delta region. Most powerful of these Fula states was the Macina Empire, centered to the southwest. The Toucouleur Empire conquered the area in the mid 19th century, and the French captured the town in 1894.\nGet in.\nIt lies 35 km to the northwest of the Niger river town of Dir\u00e9, and 97 km by highway west-southwest of Timbuktu."}
{"id": "165516", "revid": "195012", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=165516", "title": "Kidal", "text": "Kidal is a city in Mali, and the capital of the Kidal Region.\nUnderstand.\nMany of the beautiful, historic sites in Kidal were badly damaged in the 2012 Northern Mali conflict. The city is still within the influence of Tuareg rebels."}
{"id": "165519", "revid": "155858", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=165519", "title": "S\u00e9var\u00e9", "text": "S\u00e9var\u00e9 is a city in Mali's Mopti Region.\nIt is a crossroads town of about 40,000. It lies on the main route through Mali between Gao in the east, and Segou and Bamako in the west. Many travellers pass through S\u00e9var\u00e9 en route to Mopti and Bandiagara. \nUnderstand.\nSaid to have been founded by a Fula hunter, Se Waware, its importance began to grow with the construction of roads and the dike to Mopti under French colonial rule.\nGet in.\nMopti Airport is near S\u00e9var\u00e9\nS\u00e9var\u00e9 is about 10 km (6.2 mi) southeast of Mopti and 10 km (6.2 mi) south of Fatoma. From S\u00e9var\u00e9 you can access Bandiagara to the east, Gao and Timbuktu to the north, Segou and Bamako to the southwest and Burkina Faso to the south.\nYou can also reach Timbuktu by the river."}
{"id": "165520", "revid": "36420", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=165520", "title": "Hombori", "text": "Hombori is a town in the Mopti Region of Mali.\nDo.\nYou can hike the Mount Hombori Mountain and visit stone houses"}
{"id": "165521", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=165521", "title": "Bilhorod-Dnistrovskyi", "text": "Bilhorod-Dnistrovskyi (Ukrainian: \u0411\u0456\u043b\u0433\u043e\u0440\u043e\u0434-\u0414\u043d\u0456\u0441\u0442\u0440\u043e\u0432\u0441\u044c\u043a\u0438\u0439, also transliterated Belgorod-Dnestrovsky from Russian: \u0411\u0435\u043b\u0433\u043e\u0440\u043e\u0434-\u0414\u043d\u0435\u0441\u0442\u0440\u043e\u0432\u0441\u043a\u0438\u0439) is a city in Southern Ukraine, formerly known as Akkerman.\nGet in.\nBy bus.\nBuses from Odesa operate daily.\nTickets are available on BlaBlaCar. "}
{"id": "165534", "revid": "235382", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=165534", "title": "Aiserey", "text": "Aiserey is a town in the C\u00f4te-d'Or department in Bourgogne-Franche-Comt\u00e9.\nSee.\nThe attractions in Aiserey are a chateau and a castle located in the heart of the village. There are some 19th-century farm buildings listed as a historical monument."}
