{"id": "166532", "revid": "2164467", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=166532", "title": "Kilis", "text": "Kilis is a town in Southeastern Anatolia, just north of the Syrian border. In 2023 its population was 131,000. \nUnderstand.\nThere are many traces of ancient civilisations here, but Kilis was a small town until the 18th / 19th centuries, when it grew based on agricultural industries. The Ottomans governed this area from Aleppo, so after their defeat in the First World War it initially formed part of French-occupied Syria. But the peace treaty awarded it to the new Republic of Turkey, so the French withdrew and Turkish forces claimed it on 7 Dec 1921. That treaty drew the new border with Syria some 5 km south of Kilis. \nThe border continued to blight the town because of interminable wars and civil strife in Syria. There was smuggling and illegal crossings, from 2012 to 2019 a large refugee camp stood at \u00d6nc\u00fcp\u0131nar on the border, and ISIL fired rockets into Kilis. The turmoil was winding down when on 6 Feb 2023 the region on both sides was devasted by an earthquake. Many of the city's historic buildings and landmarks are badly damaged or have collapsed, and reconstruction is still under way.\nGet in.\nKilis is on Highway D-850 from Gaziantep (58 km) toward the Syrian border at \u00d6nc\u00fcp\u0131nar / Al-Salameh. The crossing is open 24 hours and the road continues south as A2 to A'zaz and Aleppo. \nHighway D-410 runs west from Kilis to join D-825 from Kahramanmara\u015f to Antakya. \n\u00d6zg\u00fcr Koop run dolmu\u015fes from Gaziantep, which set off whenever they fill up and take about an hour. \n is on Mercidab\u0131k Cd, 1 km south of city centre.\nGet around.\nDolmuses and taxis ply the main streets.\nBuy.\nSmall supermarkets cluster downtown. The most central is Bim at Cumhuriyet Cd 200, open daily 08:00-21:00.\nEat.\nEating places are central, in the blocks north of Cumhuriyet Cd. They include Do\u011fan Han Yemek, Kabalti Cafe, Tavac\u0131 \u0130brahim Usta, Aspava Cafe, Muzzo and Tavac\u0131 \u0130zzet Usta. They're mostly open daily 08:00-22:00 or later.\nDrink.\nTap water is not safe to drink, stick to bottled.\nNo free-standing bars, some cafes may serve alcohol. \nThere's a cluster of cafes northwest edge of town near the University, where Do\u011fan G\u00fcre\u015f Pa\u015fa Blv joins Highway D-410.\nLearn.\nKilis 7 Aral\u0131k University main campus is northwest edge of town, with Karata\u015f campus downtown. Founded in 2007, it has 11,000 students and 250 teaching staff. \"Aral\u0131k\" means December and the name commemorates the city's liberation from French occupation on 7 Dec 1921.\nConnect.\nAs of Sep 2026 Kilis has 5G from all Turkish carriers, but the signal is patchy along the approach roads. The Syrian networks don't reach beyond the border."}
{"id": "166536", "revid": "144844", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=166536", "title": "Concordia (Argentina)", "text": "Concordia is an excellent example of a flourishing city in Entre Rios, located at 440 kilometres from Buenos Aires. It lies on the western shore of the Uruguay River, on the border with Uruguay.\nUnderstand.\nConcordia has a population of over 172,000 inhabitants. It is also the second most populated city in Entre R\u00edos. The population in this region was made up of immigrants: Italian, Spanish, German and Jewish.\nGet in.\nThere are buses from Buenos Aires.\nThe smaller city of Salto, Uruguay, right across the river, is accessible by a bridge over Salto Grande Dam, north of the city.\nSee.\nIn this bicentennial city, there is the \"San Carlos Palace\". This place inspired Saint-Exup\u00e9ry to write part of his famous book \"The Little Prince.\" \nThere are diverse cultural sites to visit such as the Jewish Museum, the Arruabarrena House and the Ethnological and Anthropological Museum. \nOther tourist attractions are its hot springs and its rivers and lakes, where people can go fishing. At Salto Grande Dam there are plenty of dorado fish."}
{"id": "166543", "revid": "2282829", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=166543", "title": "Calatagan", "text": "Calatagan is a municipality in Batangas.\nUnderstand.\nThe town derives from the Tagalog word \"latag\", or \"plain\".\nGet in.\nNasugbu, the main town of the region, is 35 km to the north. There is a highway and jeepneys travel it."}
{"id": "166555", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=166555", "title": "Angono", "text": "Angono (pronounced as \u201cah-NGAW-naw\u201d) is a town in Rizal, Philippines. Now part of Manila's ever-expanding sprawl, no population data for this page, but it keeps its small town feel; most visitors will be drawn by its annual Higantes Festival, where papier-mache effigies are paraded around the town. Home to two renowned Filipino artists, the town is promoted as the \"Art Capital of the Philippines\"\nUnderstand.\nAngono has long been inhabited since the prehistoric era, as evidenced by the Stone Age petroglyphs found at the highland boundary with Binangonan. The fact that these primitive works of art were discovered by Carlos \u201cBotong\u201d Francisco, a local resident who is recognized as one of the Philippines\u2019 national artists for his mural paintings, further underscores Angono\u2019s reputation as the art capital of the Philippines.\nGet in.\nAngono is 28 km away from Manila, it will take 48 minutes to drive from Manila to Angono by car. Manila East Road goes through the area on both sides.\nAngono is the terminus of route 3 of Metro Manila's bus network, running from Quiapo in Manila, passing by San Juan and Pasig. They enter downtown and then turn back to Quiapo via the bypass road. Buses are either yellow or green, and mostly high-floor coaches.\nThere are jeepneys running from Cubao in Quezon City and Starmall in Mandaluyong area to Angono. The jeepneys from Quezon City usually pass through Felix Avenue in Marikina or Antipolo before joining Manila East Road, while those from Starmall use Manila East Road and pass through Cainta and Taytay."}
{"id": "166573", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=166573", "title": "Dequincy", "text": "Dequincy is a small town in Calcasieu Parish in Southwest Louisiana, about 30 minutes northwest of Lake Charles. The town's claim to fame is its railroad heritage and maintains one of the largest museums in Louisiana that is strictly dedicated to railroad history."}
