{"id": "166710", "revid": "2433524", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=166710", "title": "Alona Beach", "text": "Alona Beach is on Panglao Island in Bohol province, Philippines. It is both the island and the province's most popular resort area, and one of the top ones for the country as a whole.\nUnderstand.\nAlona is in Tawara Barangay, Panglao Municipalty. The island is not large, so anything in Tawara is quite close \u2014 less than two km away.\nThere are numerous restaurants and hotels along the beach and many others only a short distance away. The beach itself is less than a mile long; everything is within walking distance.\nGet in.\nSee the Panglao Island article for how to reach the island. Many travellers arriving by ferry go directly to Alona from the port in Tagbilaran without stopping elsewhere. Visitors arriving by air land at Panglao Airport () which is within a few km of Alona. In either case tricycles are the most-used form of ground transport to the beach area.\nBy car.\nThe Panglao Island Circumferential Road, as its name implies, goes around Panglao Island, and it connects Panglao Town and Dauis with Alona Beach. Coming from Tagbilaran, take the bridge to Dauis then turn left onto the part of the circumferential road that goes to the southern side of the island. The drive is a few miles, since Alona Beach is on the southwest corner of Panglao Island and Dauis is on the east side of the island.\nGet around.\nWithin the Alona Beach area, everything is walkable; the beach is less than a mile in length.\nAnywhere else on the island, or nearby Tagbilaran, is usually reached by jeepney or tricycle; these can be found near the middle of the beach where a road comes in. There are also organised tours using boats for dive sites and buses for scenic destinations.\nEat.\nThere are about twenty restaurants and bars along the sea front at Alona Beach, nearly all with international menus and mid-to-high prices.\nDrink.\nThere are about twenty bars along Alona Beach; most serve food as well as drink and several have live music in the evenings. It is a tourist area and there are no real bargains, but prices are quite moderate by international standards; most drinks are under \u20b1100.\nSleep.\nIn the 20th century, Alona was mainly a backpacker destination and there is still some cheap accommodation aimed at that market. However since then there has been extensive development and today there are many mid-range places and a few luxury hotels.\nAt early-2026 exchange rates \u20b12000 is under $40 US, so even some of the places listed below as \"splurge\" are cheap by international standards.\nConnect.\nMost of the hotels and more-or-less all the bars or restaurants offer free WiFi, usually reasonably fast.\nGo next.\nAnywhere on Panglao Island is readily accessible by jeepney or tricycle. Some possibilities are:\nFor places off the island, see the go next section of the island article."}
{"id": "166711", "revid": "2361892", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=166711", "title": "Dauis", "text": "Dauis is on Panglao Island in Bohol, Phlippines.\nUnderstand.\nThe municipality of Dauis is fairly small and just across a bridge from the provincial capital, Tagbilaran. It includes about half the island and is 3 km from Tagbilaran.\nGet in.\nSee the Panglao Island article for how to reach the island. Dauis is the closest part of the island to Tagbilaran, just across a bridge."}
{"id": "166712", "revid": "2361892", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=166712", "title": "Panglao Town", "text": "Panglao Town is on Panglao Island in Bohol, Philippines.\nUnderstand.\nThe town is fairly small and covers half the island sharing with the municipality of Dauis.\nThe popular resort area Alona Beach is administratively part of Panglao Municipality but has its own Wikivoyage article.\nMomo Beach is just outside Panglao Town and is covered in this article. It is less developed and less hectic than Alona.\nGet in.\nSee the Panglao Island article for how to reach the island, including Panglao Town."}
{"id": "166745", "revid": "2183461", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=166745", "title": "Danao Beach", "text": "Danao Beach is on Panglao Island in the Bohol province of the Philippines."}
{"id": "166751", "revid": "2240936", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=166751", "title": "Bolod and Libaong", "text": "Bolod and Libaong are barangays on Panglao Island in the Philippines, east of the island's main tourist area Alona Beach."}
{"id": "166754", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=166754", "title": "Anning", "text": "Anning (\u5b89\u5b81, \u0100nn\u00edng) is in Kunming Prefecture.\nUnderstand.\nThe Chinese city of Anning is approximately one hour's bus ride outside of Kunming. It is well-known for the Jinfang Hot Spring, which feature colored bathing pools that are scented with different aromas, such as strawberry, coconut, and rose. The hot springs also feature bathing and changing areas, as well as a pond of cleaner fish that eat the dead skin off the feat and legs of visitors.\nGet in.\nAs of 2019, the best method for reaching Anning was by bus from Kunming's West Bus Station. The journey time from there is about one hour. Buses to Anning are also available from the bus station outside the Kunming South Railway Station."}
{"id": "166765", "revid": "144844", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=166765", "title": "Bafang", "text": "Bafang is in the Northwest Highlands region of Cameroon, located in the territorial heart of the Bamileke people."}
{"id": "166772", "revid": "2200674", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=166772", "title": "Tibasosa", "text": "Tibasosa is a village in the Boyac\u00e1 department of Colombia.\nUnderstand.\nThis is an agricultural town close to Sogamoso with pleasant weather. It was founded in 1778 and is known as the capital of the feijoa, as it produces large amounts of this fruit and derived products. The largest zoo in the region is right outside of town.\nGet in.\nFrequent buses from Sogamoso (10 km, 3000 COP)\nGet around.\nTibasosa is a small enough to see on foot.\nSee.\nThe area around the main plaza.\nBuy.\nMany products made from feijoa including desserts, jams, sweets and liqueurs.\nGo next.\nIf you don't want to backtrack to Sogamoso, consider visiting Duitama, Paipa or Nobsa"}
{"id": "166779", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=166779", "title": "Xilitla", "text": "Xilitla is a small town of 6,500 people (2010) in San Luis Potos\u00ed whose main claim to fame is the somewhat bizarre artwork that British artist Edward James left and created here. It is also known for its mountainous landscapes and its springs.\nUnderstand.\nWhile Xilitla (pronounced with a leading Spanish j sound) has been mentioned in 16th century Spanish documents and bears an indigenous name roughly meaning \"place of snails\", it would likely be just another delightful yet forgettable mountain town in the interior of Mexico if not for one eccentric wealthy British artist, who got inspired by the local environment to put his surrealist art into practice. He was initially drawn here to build an orchid garden, but a particularly fierce winter killed 15,000 plants, convinced him to instead build something more permanent: a bizarre mixture of sculptures and garden which manages at the same time to integrate itself into the semi-artificial nature and be a parody on modern architecture. Few places outside the Washington D.C.metro have ever made \"naked concrete\" so interesting to look at.\nThe economy is primarily agricultural with the main crops being coffee and citrus. The lack of industrialization has helped preserve the Nahuatl and Teenek indigenous cultures and their traditional ways of life.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nThe old \"carretera\" linking Mexico city with the US border passes through town and while its long distance travel function has been largely overtaken by newer \"autopistas\" it still serves as an important freight and local corridor. Beware of omnipresent \"topes\", curvy mountainous roads and reckless overtaking maneuvers. Some stretches of the road are affected by various kinds of crime, but you should be fine driving during daylight hours and not stopping in the most dangerous parts.\nBy bus.\nThere are daily direct buses to Mexico city and other major cities, but more frequent connections open up if you're willing to change into a \"colectivo\" minivan at \"La Y Griega\", a major T junction, literally named \"the Y\".\nFrom Ciudad de Valles goes Frontera for about M$118 (Jan 2022). You can also check Grupo Vencedor.\nGet around.\nWalking is the best way to get around the core downtown even though there are steep, cobblestone streets. Taxis are plentiful and are very affordable, offering the most flexible way to get around the town. Uber does not operate in Xilitla. Combis are shared van services that operates along a fixed route, there is a combi that goes from downtown to the \"Las Pozas\" surrealist sculpture garden.\nEat.\nA local food specialty is the \"zacahuil\" (also spelled \"sacahuil\"), a giant tamale (3 to 4 feet long) wrapped in banana leaves and filled with pork on one side and chicken on the other. Zacahuil is a traditional Huastec food and is seldom found outside the \"Huasteca\" region."}
{"id": "166783", "revid": "36420", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=166783", "title": "Carr\u00edcola", "text": "Carr\u00edcola is in the Valencia region of Spain. Carr\u00edcola is a tiny village with a population of 100 people. It styles itself as \"La Xispa de la Vall\" (the Spark of the Valley)."}
{"id": "166820", "revid": "235382", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=166820", "title": "Minle", "text": "Minle (\u6c11\u4e50\u53bf), sometimes transliterated Minyue, is a county in Zhangye prefecture, in Gansu province, China. \nUnderstand.\nWith rapeflower fields bordered by the Qilian Mountains on the south, Minle can look spectacular mid-summer. The rest of the year, it may be less interesting. There is a small city a few km southwest of the train station, but the rest of the county is basically rural. \nGet in.\nHighway G227 between Zhangye and Xining passes through Minle.\nSleep.\nOnly a handful of hotels here are authorized to take foreigners, and the authorities do check. Expect to surrender your passport to the front desk for an hour or two so the police can come over to inspect it.\nGo next.\nZhangye\nXining"}
